WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.640
 Hello, and welcome to another walkthrough for Computer Architecture for Everybody. We're continuing on our

00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:13.700
 CDC 6504 emulator, and now we're going to load our most complex sample. So let's take a look at its assembly language.

00:00:13.700 --> 00:00:20.840
 Here we have it, and let's move it over here, and let's move it up so we see all of the instructions. There we go.

00:00:20.840 --> 00:00:23.460
 We can just barely see all the instructions. This is our first thing.

00:00:23.460 --> 00:00:28.300
 That's so long that we're gonna end up in the second column of the instructions. So to start at what we've got here,

00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:32.980
 we got a data pseudo operation that fills the data memory with the string "hello"

00:00:32.980 --> 00:00:37.680
 H-E-L-L-O with H being uppercase already and E-L-L-O. When we're done,

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:43.500
 we want all these characters to be uppercase, and so we're gonna write a loop that's going to go through

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:50.300
 from 0 until it's, and it's gonna stop at a 0 byte, and it's gonna load the value, check if it's already uppercase.

00:00:50.300 --> 00:00:56.700
 If it's not, it's gonna subtract 20 to it, which we did before because that's the mapping between uppercase and lowercase,

00:00:56.700 --> 00:01:01.200
 and then we're gonna store it back in. So when we're done, 48 won't change because that's already uppercase.

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:07.300
 65 will become 45, 6C will become 4C, etc., and then it'll stop and it'll print out the uppercase.

00:01:07.300 --> 00:01:12.380
 So this is just kind of like an uppercase method in Python or whatever, okay?

00:01:12.380 --> 00:01:18.660
 So let's take a look at the code. We have a top of a loop, and we have a jump to the top of the loop at the bottom.

00:01:18.660 --> 00:01:25.440
 We're using X as our iteration variable, X, because we have this really cool load that says I want to load from 0,

00:01:25.900 --> 00:01:28.140
 offset by the current value in X.

00:01:28.140 --> 00:01:34.640
 Okay, so X is gonna start at 0 because we're gonna clear it, and then we're gonna slowly but surely add X.

00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:37.980
 So this is like X equals X plus 1 each time through the loop.

00:01:37.980 --> 00:01:44.500
 So this is the bottom of the loop, kind of the continue area, where we're saying X plus plus or X equals X plus 1 to

00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:51.120
 advance X through the string because we're gonna use X as the array offset as it's going through our little array.

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:57.620
 We're gonna load the value, offset by X, and then we're gonna immediately check to see if we got a 0 byte,

00:01:57.620 --> 00:02:00.860
 and that's how we terminate this loop when we hit a 0 byte.

00:02:00.860 --> 00:02:04.120
 So we only want to go through memory until we hit a 0 byte, okay?

00:02:04.120 --> 00:02:07.380
 So we don't want to run out of memory or blow up or run into something else.

00:02:07.380 --> 00:02:13.780
 We stop at a 0 byte, and then we're gonna check to see if the letter we just got is already uppercase.

00:02:13.780 --> 00:02:18.020
 If it is, that means it's less than lowercase a. That's how ASCII works, right?

00:02:18.020 --> 00:02:20.020
 So let me go ahead and show you the ASCII stuff.

00:02:20.940 --> 00:02:26.280
 [silence]

00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:37.160
 The lowercase characters are higher by a factor of 20 hex, so a, lowercase a is 61, and uppercase a is 41.

00:02:37.160 --> 00:02:42.900
 You subtract 0 X 20 to get from lowercase to upper, and our code is going to upper.

00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:50.860
 So we're checking to see if we are above or below lowercase a, if we're below lowercase a already,

00:02:50.860 --> 00:02:54.880
 we're gonna do a branch minus down here and then increment to go to the next one.

00:02:54.880 --> 00:03:00.960
 If, on the other hand, we are above the letter a, then we're going to subtract 20,

00:03:00.960 --> 00:03:04.760
 and then we're gonna store that using our index again as X.

00:03:04.760 --> 00:03:07.100
 So X is really cool. It's just gonna be our counter.

00:03:07.100 --> 00:03:12.580
 And so this register is kind of like the iteration variable. Well, it is the iteration variable of our loop.

00:03:12.580 --> 00:03:17.960
 So let's go ahead and get started. So I'll turn on narration of execution, line ourselves up here,

00:03:17.960 --> 00:03:19.960
 take our first step, clear the X register.

00:03:19.960 --> 00:03:24.320
 PC 0X 00, clear X register.

00:03:24.320 --> 00:03:31.600
 So now we're at the top of the loop, and we're gonna do a load from memory location 0, offset by X register.

00:03:31.600 --> 00:03:37.480
 The X register is actually part of this instruction, okay? So let's go ahead and load that.

00:03:37.480 --> 00:03:44.320
 PC 0X 01, load accumulator from address 0 and X with X.

00:03:44.320 --> 00:03:50.420
 Okay, so now you see that the accumulator is 0X 48, and that is the uppercase hello.

00:03:50.420 --> 00:03:56.920
 And that load set the Z flag and the N flag, and it's neither 0 nor negative.

00:03:56.920 --> 00:04:01.600
 And so let's go ahead and run the if test to see if we're done with the loop.

00:04:01.600 --> 00:04:07.840
 PC 0X 03, branch if equal address 0X 10.

00:04:07.840 --> 00:04:13.680
 Now 0X 10 would have been this exit of the loop, but it didn't because we're not minus yet.

00:04:13.680 --> 00:04:21.780
 And so that's our top tested loop, and now what we're gonna do is we're gonna say if this is greater than A,

00:04:21.780 --> 00:04:27.980
 if it's less than A, we're gonna skip these next two lines, and the next two lines are a subtract of 0X 20,

00:04:27.980 --> 00:04:33.960
 and then an index store into memory location 0 offset by X, but X is still position 0.

00:04:33.960 --> 00:04:40.720
 So this is the letter H, and it is already uppercase, so we're gonna skip around this subtraction calculation.

00:04:40.720 --> 00:04:47.160
 PC 0X 05, compare accumulator with immediate 0X 61.

00:04:47.160 --> 00:04:54.800
 And now we're gonna branch because it is N, so we're gonna branch if minus, and that means we're already uppercase.

00:04:54.800 --> 00:05:00.920
 PC 0X 07, branch if minus address 0X 0D.

00:05:00.920 --> 00:05:08.240
 So it went to 0X 0D, which skipped around the subtraction, and now we're at the kind of continue or the loop bottom,

00:05:08.240 --> 00:05:12.680
 in the increment part of the loop, and we're gonna add 1 to X.

00:05:12.680 --> 00:05:21.280
 PC 0X 0D, increment X register PC 0X 0E, jump to address 1.

00:05:21.280 --> 00:05:27.920
 Oh, it went and did it automatically. So we went up to the top of the loop, and I didn't catch it fast enough,

00:05:27.920 --> 00:05:35.280
 so it already went, and X is now 1, so it's loading memory location 1 into the accumulator.

00:05:35.280 --> 00:05:42.920
 Okay, and so the accumulator now is non-zero, so we're gonna check to see if the loop is done.

00:05:42.920 --> 00:05:49.120
 PC 0X 03, branch if equal address 0X 10.

00:05:49.120 --> 00:05:54.440
 And it didn't, so now we're gonna check to see the case of our current character.

00:05:54.440 --> 00:06:00.880
 PC 0X 05, compare accumulator with immediate 0X 61.

00:06:00.880 --> 00:06:06.920
 So that is checking to see if it's above or below A, and this one is above A,

00:06:06.920 --> 00:06:13.800
 and so we're gonna have to map it into uppercase, so you'll notice that this BMI does not jump to the CONT label.

00:06:13.800 --> 00:06:15.040
 This one falls through.

00:06:15.040 --> 00:06:20.760
 PC 0X 07, branch if minus address 0X 0D.

00:06:20.760 --> 00:06:26.520
 So now we're at 0X 09, not 0X 0D, and now we're gonna do the subtract of 20,

00:06:26.520 --> 00:06:29.920
 and then we're gonna store it into the memory.

00:06:29.920 --> 00:06:35.880
 PC 0X 09, subtract with carry immediate 0X 20.

00:06:35.880 --> 00:06:37.600
 And now we're gonna store it.

00:06:37.600 --> 00:06:43.600
 PC 0X 0B, store accumulator to address 0 and X with X.

00:06:43.600 --> 00:06:49.120
 So now in location 1, we took this 45 that was our calculated uppercase character,

00:06:49.120 --> 00:06:53.760
 and now it has been stored in position 1.

00:06:53.760 --> 00:06:58.280
 And so the next thing we do is add 1 and then jump to the top of the loop.

00:06:58.280 --> 00:07:03.120
 PC 0X 0D, increment X register.

00:07:03.120 --> 00:07:07.280
 PC 0X 0E, jump to address 1.

00:07:07.280 --> 00:07:08.680
 So now we're at the top of the loop.

00:07:08.680 --> 00:07:11.480
 We're gonna grab the next character, 'cause X is now 2,

00:07:11.480 --> 00:07:14.480
 so we're gonna grab the 2 offset from our array.

00:07:14.480 --> 00:07:20.760
 PC 0X 01, load accumulator from address 0 and X with X.

00:07:20.760 --> 00:07:25.360
 So we got 60, so I'm gonna just run this a bit, okay?

00:07:25.360 --> 00:07:30.120
 And I'll try to stop it so that I can show you kind of the last iteration of the loop.

00:07:30.120 --> 00:07:31.520
 So we'll watch X.

00:07:31.520 --> 00:07:36.360
 I'll probably try to catch it while X sub, the fourth iteration,

00:07:36.360 --> 00:07:39.120
 the, well, the fifth iteration, but sub 4 is running.

00:07:39.120 --> 00:07:43.120
 Let's see if I can start it and then catch it.

00:07:43.120 --> 00:07:48.720
 PC 0X 03, branch of equal address 0X 10.

00:07:48.720 --> 00:07:55.280
 PC 0X 05, compare accumulator with immediate 0X 61.

00:07:55.280 --> 00:08:01.600
 PC 0X 07, branch of minus address 0X 0D.

00:08:01.600 --> 00:08:06.080
 We're on a series of things that have to be converted to uppercase now.

00:08:06.080 --> 00:08:12.200
 PC 0X 0B, store accumulator to address 0 and X with X.

00:08:12.200 --> 00:08:16.320
 PC 0X 0D, increment X register.

00:08:16.320 --> 00:08:20.200
 PC 0X 0E, jump to address 1.

00:08:20.200 --> 00:08:25.080
 PC 0X 01, load accumulator from address 0

00:08:25.080 --> 00:08:26.400
 and X with X.

00:08:26.400 --> 00:08:32.000
 PC 0X 03, branch of equal address 0X 10.

00:08:32.000 --> 00:08:38.520
 PC 0X 05, compare accumulator with immediate 0X 61.

00:08:38.520 --> 00:08:44.280
 PC 0X 07, branch of minus address 0X 0D.

00:08:44.280 --> 00:08:50.240
 PC 0X 09, subtract with carry immediate 0X 20.

00:08:50.240 --> 00:08:55.040
 PC 0X 0B, store accumulator to address 0

00:08:55.040 --> 00:08:56.380
 and X with X.

00:08:56.380 --> 00:09:00.520
 PC 0X 0D, increment X register.

00:09:00.520 --> 00:09:04.360
 PC 0X 0E, jump to address 1.

00:09:04.360 --> 00:09:09.200
 PC 0X 01, load accumulator from address 0

00:09:09.200 --> 00:09:10.560
 and X with X.

00:09:10.560 --> 00:09:16.160
 PC 0X 03, branch of equal address 0X 10.

00:09:16.160 --> 00:09:22.680
 PC 0X 05, compare accumulator with immediate 0X 61.

00:09:22.680 --> 00:09:28.400
 PC 0X 07, branch of minus address 0X 0D.

00:09:28.400 --> 00:09:31.080
 PC 0X 0--

00:09:31.080 --> 00:09:32.560
 OK, so I kind of stopped it.

00:09:32.560 --> 00:09:33.600
 So where are we at here?

00:09:33.600 --> 00:09:36.840
 We're about to store this last character.

00:09:36.840 --> 00:09:39.440
 We got it subtracted, so it's now uppercase.

00:09:39.440 --> 00:09:42.920
 And we're about to store it into position offset by X.

00:09:42.920 --> 00:09:45.200
 Our iteration variable is 4.

00:09:45.200 --> 00:09:52.560
 PC 0X 0B, store accumulator to address 0 and X with X.

00:09:52.560 --> 00:09:55.680
 The next thing we're going to do is increment X.

00:09:55.680 --> 00:09:57.520
 So X is going to become 5.

00:09:57.520 --> 00:10:02.280
 PC 0X 0D, increment X register.

00:10:02.280 --> 00:10:04.440
 And now we're going to jump to the top of the loop.

00:10:04.440 --> 00:10:08.240
 PC 0X 0E, jump to address 1.

00:10:08.240 --> 00:10:09.920
 So now we're at the top of the loop.

00:10:09.920 --> 00:10:11.440
 So X is 5.

00:10:11.440 --> 00:10:15.240
 And we're going to load the character at offset 5.

00:10:15.240 --> 00:10:18.120
 And so now you'll notice, as soon as we do this,

00:10:18.120 --> 00:10:21.920
 accumulator is going to be 0 and the Z flag is going to be set.

00:10:21.920 --> 00:10:28.040
 PC 0X 01, load accumulator from address 0 and X with X.

00:10:28.040 --> 00:10:31.640
 So see, you got accumulator of 0 and we got Z.

00:10:31.640 --> 00:10:35.680
 So now we are going to do a BEQ to done.

00:10:35.680 --> 00:10:38.520
 So we're going to jump right out of this loop

00:10:38.520 --> 00:10:42.280
 because now we've hit the 0 byte in our character array.

00:10:42.280 --> 00:10:49.160
 PC 0X 03, branch if equal address 0X 1 0.

00:10:49.160 --> 00:10:49.760
 One more.

00:10:49.760 --> 00:10:55.600
 PC 0X 1 0, break printed output, hello.

00:10:55.600 --> 00:10:56.680
 So it finished.

00:10:56.680 --> 00:10:57.800
 It finished.

00:10:57.800 --> 00:11:01.120
 And look, it printed out an uppercase version

00:11:01.120 --> 00:11:03.200
 of the word hello.

00:11:03.200 --> 00:11:06.280
 So I recommend that you go in and you play with this

00:11:06.280 --> 00:11:09.520
 and you load this and you control the reset, the narrate,

00:11:09.520 --> 00:11:10.280
 the stepping.

00:11:10.280 --> 00:11:11.960
 You kind of see how I'm doing it.

00:11:11.960 --> 00:11:13.800
 I'll tell you, I know what I'm doing

00:11:13.800 --> 00:11:15.600
 and it's really hard to read these things.

00:11:15.600 --> 00:11:17.120
 And that's why I kind of think of this

00:11:17.120 --> 00:11:18.760
 from an assembly language perspective.

00:11:18.760 --> 00:11:19.880
 I'm trying to figure out.

00:11:19.880 --> 00:11:22.160
 It doesn't hurt to sort of hover over and say,

00:11:22.160 --> 00:11:23.640
 oh, that's the compare instruction

00:11:23.640 --> 00:11:25.120
 and that is the letter A.

00:11:25.120 --> 00:11:27.120
 And you can hover over these things

